# NUMERICS: `>>` is a LOGICAL shift and a shift count is taken modulo 64 — and
# the folded form of a shift must give the same answer as the emitted one.
#
# WHY THE FIRST TWO NUMBERS ARE THE SAME BITS TWICE. `a` shifts a value the
# emitter cannot read; `b` shifts the identical bit pattern written as a
# literal, which the constant folder consumes. The folder was already unsigned
# and the emit was a signed C `>>`, so ONE operator answered two things in one
# program: measured, `a` printed -4 and `b` printed 9223372036854775804. A
# constant is not allowed to mean something different from the value it is.
#
# WHY 64 AND 65 ARE HERE. C leaves a shift at or above the width undefined and
# clang used the licence: `1 << 64` and `1 << 65` both emitted the value of an
# unrelated earlier binding. Both AArch64 and x86-64 mask the count to 6 bits,
# so that is what the emitted program must do to agree with the machine it
# lowers to. `examples/table/shifts.id` states the same rule from the other end.
#
# Computed by hand: -8 is 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF8; logically shifted right one place
# that is 0x7FFFFFFFFFFFFFFC = 9223372036854775804. 1 << (64 & 63) = 1.
# 1 << (65 & 63) = 2. 1 << 63 = 0x8000000000000000, read as i64 the minimum.
# expect: 9223372036854775804 9223372036854775804 1 2 -9223372036854775808
# @corpus current
n = 8
bits = 0 - n
a = bits >> 1
b = 18446744073709551608 >> 1
one = 1
c = one << 64
d = one << 65
e = one << 63
print("{a} {b} {c} {d} {e}")
